	"# Flying\n"
	"## HyperSpace\n"
	"HyperSpace is the red void between the stars. Each star system appears as a glowing dot; fly into one to enter that system. Flying burns fuel, and running out of fuel in HyperSpace leaves you stranded, so keep an eye on the green ring on the dashboard map: it shows how far your fuel can take you.\n"
	"\n"
	"## Setting a course\n"
	"1. Tap MAP.\n"
	"2. Tap a star. Its name, distance and the fuel it takes appear on the right. FUEL turns red if you can't make it.\n"
	"3. Tap the same star again, or SET COURSE. The map closes and the autopilot flies you there.\n"
	"\n"
	"On the map, drag to pan, + and - zoom, SHIP centers on your ship. The course stays on the dashboard until you arrive or steer yourself.\n"
	"\n"
	"## Solar systems\n"
	"Inside a system you see the sun, the planets and their orbits. Fly into a planet to go into orbit around it; fly into its moons from there. Fly off the edge of the screen to leave the system for HyperSpace.\n"
	"\n"
	"## QuasiSpace\n"
	"Later in the game you may find a way into QuasiSpace, a green void with very different distances. There is no galaxy map there.\n"
	"\n"
	"# The ship menu\n"
	"Tap MENU (or press B) while flying. The ship menu appears as big buttons; the highlighted one is the game's current choice.\n"
	"| Starmap | The game's own starmap. The MAP tab is quicker.\n"
	"| Devices | Use alien devices you have found.\n"
	"| Cargo | Minerals, life data and fuel on board.\n"
	"| Roster | Your escort ships and their crew.\n"
	"| Game | Save, Load, Quit, Settings.\n"
	"| Navigate | Close the menu and keep flying.\n"
	"\n"
	"Some menu styles group Devices, Cargo and Roster under Manifest. The buttons work either way.\n"
	"\n"
	"## Saving\n"
	"START, then Save game, then pick a slot (or MENU, Game, Save Game: the same list). Save often, especially before a fight or a long trip.\n"
	"\n"
	"## The exit save\n"
	"Slot 50 is kept for the exit save (named Exit save). Exit game in the START menu saves there first, then leaves, while you are flying in HyperSpace or in a star system. It does not save in a menu, in a conversation, in combat or in Super Melee; there it says \"Not saved here: unsaved progress is lost.\" The question is \"Exit Ur-Quan Masters?\" with YES on the left and NO on the right; NO is the default. Closing the game from the HOME menu (HOME, then Close) makes the same save first, waiting up to 6 seconds for it.\n"
	"\n"
	"Slot 50 keeps its previous copy: the exit save is written beside it, and if the write fails the old Exit save is put back. If the save fails the game says \"Not saved!\" and asks \"Exit anyway? Unsaved progress is lost.\" with NO to stay; it never closes after a failed save without asking. A save counts as failed if its file cannot be closed properly (a full SD card). To come back, choose Continue on the main menu (or load slot 50 from any Load Game list). Use slots 1 to 49 for your own saves.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Planets\n"
	"## In orbit\n"
	"Orbiting a planet replaces the ship menu with the orbit menu: Scan, Starmap, Devices, Cargo, Roster, Game and Navigate. Navigate leaves orbit.\n"
	"\n"
	"## Scanning\n"
	"Scan shows the planet's surface and offers Mineral, Energy and Biological scans, Auto Scan (all three) and Dispatch Lander. Minerals show as colored dots, energy sources and life forms as their own markers.\n"
	"\n"
	"## The lander\n"
	"Dispatch Lander sends a lander down where the cursor points. On the surface:\n"
	"- Pad: steer. Y or Up: drive.\n"
	"- A: fire, to stun life forms so you can collect them.\n"
	"- Drive over minerals and stunned life to pick them up.\n"
	"- B: take off and return to your ship.\n"
	"\n"
	"Earthquakes, lightning and fire hurt the crew on board. A lander with no crew left is lost. Minerals are sold for RU at the starbase.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Talking to aliens\n"
	"When you meet a ship, choose Converse to talk or Attack to fight. Some aliens attack you anyway.\n"
	"\n"
	"During a conversation the alien speaks first. Your possible replies appear as big buttons along the bottom of the touch screen; tap one to say it.\n"
	"- While the alien talks: SKIP (or B) jumps to the end, REPLAY (or Left) repeats the last line, Right fast-forwards.\n"
	"- If there are more replies than fit, MORE REPLIES shows the rest.\n"
	"\n"
	"Listen carefully. Most of what you learn in this game, you learn by talking.\n"
	"\n"
	"# The starbase\n"
	"The starbase orbits Earth. Once it is yours you can:\n"
	"| Commander | Talk to him: advice, news and missions.\n"
	"| Outfit Starship | Buy fuel and modules for your flagship.\n"
	"| Shipyard | Hire crew and build escort ships.\n"
	"| Depart Starbase | Back to space.\n"
	"\n"
	"In Outfit and the Shipyard, Up adds and Down removes (fuel, crew), A confirms, B goes back. Money comes from selling minerals and from alien deals.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Ship combat\n"
	"Each ship has crew, energy and two abilities.\n"
	"- A: main weapon. B: special ability. Both use energy, which recharges on its own.\n"
	"- Y or Up: thrust. Pad: turn.\n"
	"- Crew is your hit points. When it reaches zero the ship is destroyed.\n"
	"- Planets pull you in with gravity, and asteroids hurt.\n"
	"- SELECT tries to retreat, when the fight allows it.\n"
	"\n"
	"In a story battle you pick which ship in your fleet fights next. Every ship plays very differently: try them all in Super Melee.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Super Melee\n"
	"Ship-against-ship battles with fleets you build.\n"
	"\n"
	"## Setting up\n"
	"- Tap a ship slot to change it: a list of all 25 ships with their point cost appears. Tap one, or EMPTY SLOT.\n"
	"- The buttons on the right of the game: Controls (Human, Weak, Good or Awesome computer), Save and Load a fleet, Battle!, Quit. Net appears for network play.\n"
	"- The dashboard lists both fleets with their points.\n"
	"\n"
	"## In battle\n"
	"Before each round, pick the ship to send in from your remaining ships, or RANDOM SHIP. The computer picks its own.\n"
	"\n"
	"To stop a battle early, hold SELECT and tap YES. Quit on the Super Melee screen goes back to the main menu.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Netplay\n"
	"Super Melee over Wi-Fi against another 3DS running this port, or against The Ur-Quan Masters 0.8.0 on a PC.\n"
	"1. Both players open Super Melee. Each taps Net on the side the other player will control.\n"
	"2. One player taps WAIT FOR CONNECTION. Their 3DS's IP address is shown on screen.\n"
	"3. The other taps HOST, types that IP, then CONNECT TO HOST. The default port is 21837.\n"
	"4. Each player sets their own side to Human, then both tap Battle!.\n"
	"\n"
	"DELAY trades responsiveness for smoothness on a slow network. Both 3DS need to be on Wi-Fi.\n"
	"\n"
	"While a side is set to Net, the HOME button and closing the lid do nothing, because either would cut the connection. Set the side back to Human or a computer player to use them again.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Settings and files\n"
	"## Settings\n"
	"Setup (main menu) changes music, speech, subtitles, the menu style (PC or 3DO) and melee zoom. Game, then Settings (ship menu) switches sound and music, sets how fast the computer fights, and renames your captain or ship.\n"
	"\n"
	"## Files on the SD card\n"
	"| save/ | Your saved games (in sdmc:/3ds/uqm/).\n"
	"| uqm.cfg | The game's settings.\n"
	"| port3ds.cfg | Which screen the game is on, FPS and left-handed play. It, like uqm.cfg, is written only when something changed, through a temporary file. If it cannot be written the top screen says \"Settings not saved\" for a few seconds.\n"
	"| uqm.log | Errors and start and exit lines, for bug reports.\n"
	"| manual.txt | Replaces this manual (see the last section).\n"
	"\n"
	"# Troubleshooting\n"
	"## There is no sound\n"
	"Run DSP1 from the Homebrew Launcher once (it writes sdmc:/3ds/dspfirm.cdc), then restart the game.\n"
	"\n"
	"## It says the game data is missing\n"
	"Send the data from the PC: install-3ds.sh with the 3DS's IP and --uqm-data.\n"
	"\n"
	"## The stylus does nothing\n"
	"Tapping flies the ship only while you are flying with no menu open. In combat the buttons do everything.\n"
	"\n"
	"## HOME does nothing in Super Melee\n"
	"A side is set to Net. Set it to Human or a computer player first (see Netplay).\n"
	"\n"
	"## The game crashed\n"
	"Look at sdmc:/3ds/uqm/uqm.log and send it with a note of what you were doing.\n"
	"\n"
	"## Where the game is\n"
	"The program is sdmc:/3ds/uqm.3dsx, directly in /3ds. The game data is in sdmc:/3ds/uqm/content/packages/ and everything else (saves, settings, log) is in sdmc:/3ds/uqm/.\n"
	"\n"
	"## Changing this manual\n"
	"A file named manual.txt in sdmc:/3ds/uqm/ replaces the built-in text. A line starting with \"# \" starts a section, \"## \" a heading, \"- \" a list item, \"1. \" a numbered step, and \"| a | b\" a two-column table row. A blank line ends a paragraph.\n"
	"\n";
